The {{Nihongo|'''Liberal Democratic Party of Okaiken'''|自由民主党|Jiyū-Minshutō}}, frequently abbreviated to '''LDP''' or {{Nihongo|'''Jimintō'''|自民党}}, is a [[conservative]] political party in [[Okaiken]]. Founded in 1977, it is one of the most successful political parties in Sanese history, having governed the country for the vast majority of the late 20th and early 21st centuries.

The party was formed from the reorganization of the [[Republican Party (Okaiken)]] under Prime Minister [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]]. It established a period of political dominance known as the "1977 System," presiding over the "Go-go 8090" economic boom. After a brief loss of power in the early 1990s following the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]], the LDP returned to govern continuously from 1993 to 2016.

The party's long reign ended in 2016 following the resignation of [[Sadakazu Tanizaki]] and a landslide defeat to the [[Sanese Communist Party]]. Since then, it has served as the primary opposition party. Under its current leader, [[Koichiro Sadanuma]], the LDP experienced a resurgence in the 2025 general election, positioning itself as a fiscally responsible alternative to the ruling [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]]-[[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] coalition.

==History==
===Formation and the "1977 System" (1977–1990)===
The LDP was officially founded on July 14, 1977, when Prime Minister [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]] reorganized the ruling [[Republican Party (Okaiken)]] to consolidate conservative forces against the rising socialist opposition. This marked the beginning of the "1977 System," an era of unparalleled political stability.

Under leaders like [[Takeo Fukuda]] and [[Junichiro Okazaki]], the LDP solidified the country's alliance with [[Shayden]] via the 1979 Security Treaty revision. The 1980s, particularly under Prime Minister [[Naoto Emi]], defined the party's economic legacy. Emi's "Doubling of National Assets" plan fueled the "Go-go 8090" economic boom, characterized by rapid deregulation and soaring asset prices.

The era reached its zenith under [[Masazumi Kato]] (1987–1989), but the bubble economy's excesses led to rampant corruption. The [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]] of 1990 implicated the cabinet of [[Kentaro Tokuyama]], forcing his resignation and severely damaging the party's credibility.

===The "Wilderness Years" (1990–1993)===
In late 1990, the LDP lost its parliamentary majority for the first time since its founding. [[Hideaki Fujio]]'s short-lived caretaker government fell to a vote of no confidence, ushering in the first non-LDP administration under [[Shigehara Honda]] of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party]] (PPO).

The LDP spent roughly two years in opposition, during which the governing coalition of the PPO, [[Komeitō]], and the [[Okaiken Shintō]] (ONP) struggled to manage the bursting of the economic bubble.

===The Long Restoration (1993–2016)=== 
Following the political chaos of the early 1990s, the LDP returned to power in February 1993 under the leadership of Isao Tomita. Tomita's administration was characterized by a concerted effort to stabilize the nation's fracturing political landscape, forming a coalition that gradually restored the LDP's single-party dominance. This marked the beginning of a twenty-three-year period of continuous rule, during which the party navigated Okaiken through the complex aftermath of the economic bubble bursting and profound shifts in the social fabric of the nation.

By the mid-1990s, the party's focus shifted from pure industrial growth to social stability. Under Prime Minister Banri Kaieda (1995–1998), the LDP enacted the landmark National Nursing Care Act of 1996. This legislation represented a fundamental transformation of the Sanese welfare state, redirecting government resources to support the rapidly aging population and cementing the LDP's support base among elderly voters. However, the economic underlying structure remained fragile. The tenure of Katsuya Okada (1998–2003) was defined by the deepening of the "Lost Decade." Okada attempted to address the stagnation through controversial banking reforms designed to clear bad debts held by "zombie" corporations, as well as an overhaul of the national education system, though these measures failed to produce an immediate economic recovery and eroded the party's popularity among the urban working class.

The early 2000s saw the LDP grapple with internal corruption and natural disasters. The administration of [[Yoshito Sengoku]] (2003–2006) was dominated by crisis management following the devastating Ise Prefecture Earthquakes of 2004, but his government ultimately collapsed under the weight of a high-profile sumo match-fixing scandal and allegations of cabinet bribery. Seeking to restore its moral authority, the party turned to [[Kenshin Hirota]] (2006–2009). The son of a convicted Class A war criminal, Hirota paradoxically led the most socially liberal administration in the party's history. He established the Truth and Reconciliation Commission to address wartime atrocities, a move that improved Okaiken's diplomatic standing in Fosia but alienated the LDP's nationalist right-wing factions.

The party's nationalist wing reasserted control in the next decade, culminating in the "Jutsu Restoration" era. Under Prime Minister [[Tadahisa Jōhei]] (2011–2012), the LDP oversaw the historic 2011 constitutional amendments that formally restored the Emperor as the Head of State, fulfilling a long-held goal of Sanese conservatives. Jōhei also integrated Okaiken into the United Sovereign Nations (USNW), realigning the nation's geopolitical stance. However, this political victory was followed by tragedy. In 2013, Prime Minister [[Sasuke Genba]], overwhelmed by the handling of a renewed economic downturn and plummeting approval ratings, committed suicide while in office.

The resulting power vacuum paved the way for the rise of [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], the youngest Prime Minister in Sanese history. Inaugurated in 2013, Kurosawa implemented a radical economic policy known as "Kuronomics," characterized by aggressive monetary easing and massive public spending. While initially popular, Kurosawa's leadership style became increasingly authoritarian. his attempts to further revise the constitution and his confrontational approach to the media polarized the electorate. By 2016, public dissatisfaction had reached a boiling point, leading to his deposition by party elders and the subsequent collapse of the LDP's long reign in the 2016 general election.

===Collapse and Opposition (2016–Present)===
By 2016, public dissatisfaction with Kurosawa's leadership led to his deposition by the party. His successor, [[Sadakazu Tanizaki]], served only 53 days. In the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 general election]], the LDP suffered a catastrophic defeat to the [[Sanese Communist Party]] led by [[Sho Aoyagi]], ending 23 years of continuous rule.

The party struggled to find its footing in opposition, suffering further losses in 2021 under the leadership of the far-right [[Hakuya Terakubo]]. In 2023, the party elected the moderate fiscal conservative [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] as president. Sadanuma's "Competence Returns" campaign in the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]] resulted in a gain of 40 seats, restoring the LDP as a formidable opposition force against the PPO government.

==Ideology and Factions==
The LDP is a "big tent" conservative party, historically balancing rapid industrialization with traditional social values.
* Kochikai (Moderate): Currently the dominant faction under [[Koichiro Sadanuma]]. Focuses on fiscal discipline, economic liberalism, and maintaining the status quo on social issues.
* Seiwakai (Nationalist): The faction of the late [[Toshiro Kurosawa]] and [[Tadahisa Jōhei]]. Advocates for a stronger military, constitutional revision to further empower the Emperor, and traditionalist education.

